    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>Opposition to apartheid joined African, Indian, coloured and white South Africans in campaigns of defiance, strikes and boycotts and, after the massacre at Sharpeville in 1960, in armed struggle.</li><li>Abroad, a worldwide movement of consumer, sports and cultural boycotts, disinvestment and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanctions">sanctions</a> made <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-africa">South Africa</a> an outcast and added economic pressure to resistance at home.</li><li>Township uprisings, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanctions">sanctions</a>, a costly stalemate and the end of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cold-war">Cold War</a> brought the government to negotiate from 1990, leading to the first democratic election in April 1994.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>Protest against the South African rugby team’s tour of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-zealand">New Zealand</a> in 1981 was so fierce that during the final Test, at Eden Park in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/auckland">Auckland</a>, a light aircraft flew low over the ground dropping flour bombs onto the pitch.</p></aside>
    </section>

