    <section class="rise-fall" id="timeline" aria-labelledby="timeline-title">
      <h2 class="profile-section-title" id="timeline-title">Timeline</h2>
      <ol class="rf-events"><li><span class="rf-year">1948</span><span class="rf-text">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/national-party-south-africa">National Party</a> wins power and begins to build apartheid into law</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1952</span><span class="rf-text">The ANC and the South African Indian Congress launch the Defiance Campaign against Unjust Laws</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1955</span><span class="rf-text">The Congress of the People adopts the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/freedom-charter">Freedom Charter</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kliptown">Kliptown</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1960</span><span class="rf-text">Police kill 69 protesters at Sharpeville, and the ANC and the PAC are banned</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1964</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nelson-mandela">Nelson Mandela</a> and seven others are sentenced to life imprisonment in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rivonia-trial">Rivonia Trial</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1976</span><span class="rf-text">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soweto-uprising">Soweto uprising</a> begins with a schoolchildren’s march against teaching in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afrikaans">Afrikaans</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1990</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/f-w-de-klerk">F. W. de Klerk</a> unbans the liberation movements, and Mandela is freed after 27 years</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1994</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-africa">South Africa</a> holds its first election open to all, and Mandela becomes president</span></li></ol>
    </section>


    <div class="profile-section-block" id="background">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Background</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/apartheid"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/apartheid.jpg" alt="Apartheid" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/apartheid">Apartheid</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:DurbanSign1989.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Guinnog, CC BY-SA 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/racial-segregation">Racial segregation</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-africa">South Africa</a> long predated apartheid. The Union of South <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a>, formed in 1910 from the British colonies and the defeated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boers">Boer</a> republics, kept the vote for white men except in the Cape, where a qualified franchise admitted some Black and coloured voters, and the Natives Land Act of 1913 confined African landownership to reserves covering about 7 per cent of the country, later raised to about 13 per cent. Pass laws controlled the movement of African men, and the mines drew migrant workers from the reserves on low wages. Educated Africans founded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/african-national-congress">South African Native National Congress</a>, later the African National Congress (ANC), in 1912 to petition for their rights, and among Indians <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahatma-gandhi">Mahatma Gandhi</a> led campaigns of passive resistance against discriminatory laws between 1906 and 1914. In 1936 the Africans of the Cape lost their place on the common voters’ roll.</p><p>In the 1940s a new generation grew impatient. The ANC Youth League, founded in 1944 by Anton Lembede, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nelson-mandela">Nelson Mandela</a>, Walter Sisulu, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oliver-tambo">Oliver Tambo</a> and others, called for mass action, and in 1946 tens of thousands of African mineworkers struck for higher wages before police drove them back to work, with loss of life. In the election of May 1948 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/national-party-south-africa">National Party</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/d-f-malan">D. F. Malan</a>, speaking for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afrikaners">Afrikaner</a> nationalism and for white fears of a growing Black urban population, won a majority of seats, though not of votes, on the programme of apartheid, “separateness”.</p><p>Over the next decade the government built apartheid into law. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/population-registration-act-1950">Population Registration Act, 1950</a>, classified every person by race; the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/group-areas-act">Group Areas Act</a> of 1950 assigned separate residential areas to each group; the Prohibition of Mixed Marriages Act of 1949 and the Immorality Act banned marriage and sex across the colour line; the Suppression of Communism Act of 1950 gave the state wide powers against its opponents; and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bantu-education-act-1953">Bantu Education Act, 1953</a>, imposed an inferior schooling on African children. Coloured voters were removed from the common roll in 1956. Under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hendrik-verwoerd">Hendrik Verwoerd</a>, prime minister from 1958, “grand apartheid” assigned every African to an ethnic homeland, or <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bantustan">Bantustan</a>, and some 3.5 million people were forcibly removed from their homes between 1960 and the early 1980s.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="aims">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Aims</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/steve-biko"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/steve-biko.jpg" alt="Steve Biko" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/steve-biko">Steve Biko</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Steve_Biko_on_Flyer_for_Steve_Biko_Memorial_at_the_Carver_Cultural_Center.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Cape Argus, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The main current of resistance sought not separate states but a single, democratic <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-africa">South Africa</a> for all its people. The ANC’s Programme of Action of 1949 called for boycotts, strikes and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/civil-disobedience">civil disobedience</a>, and in 1955 the Congress Alliance, which joined the ANC with Indian, coloured and white congresses and a trade union federation, adopted the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/freedom-charter">Freedom Charter</a>. It declared that “South <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a> belongs to all who live in it, black and white”, demanded equal rights, votes for all and the sharing of the land, and called for the mineral wealth, the banks and monopoly industry to be transferred to the ownership of the people as a whole.</p><p>Others rejected parts of this vision. The Africanists who founded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pan-africanist-congress-of-azania">Pan Africanist Congress</a> (PAC) under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/robert-sobukwe">Robert Sobukwe</a> in 1959 held that Africans must lead their own liberation and distrusted the influence of white communists in the Congress Alliance. From the late 1960s the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/black-consciousness-movement">Black Consciousness Movement</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/steve-biko">Steve Biko</a> urged Black people, a term that for Biko included Africans, Indians and coloureds, to free themselves from psychological dependence on white liberals and build their own organisations. White liberals of the Liberal Party and later the Progressive Party opposed apartheid within the law, while Mangosuthu Buthelezi’s Inkatha, founded in 1975, rejected apartheid but also armed struggle and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanctions">sanctions</a>.</p><p>Violence divided the movement. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/albert-luthuli">Albert Luthuli</a>, president of the ANC from 1952, led it in non-violent defiance rooted in Christian and Gandhian ideals, but after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sharpeville-massacre">Sharpeville massacre</a> and the banning of the ANC in 1960, Mandela and others concluded that peaceful protest had been met only with force. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/umkhonto-we-sizwe">Umkhonto we Sizwe</a>, “Spear of the Nation”, announced in December 1961 that the people now faced only two choices, to submit or to fight; it began with sabotage against installations, intended to avoid loss of life, before turning to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guerrilla-warfare">guerrilla</a> war. The government, for its part, presented apartheid as “separate development”, self-determination for each nation in its own homeland, and as a bulwark against communism, and from the late 1970s P. W. Botha met what he called a “total onslaught” with a “total strategy”. Abroad, the anti-apartheid movement sought to isolate South Africa through boycotts, sanctions and exclusion from international sport, and to support political prisoners and the liberation movements.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="events">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Course of Events</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/nelson-mandela"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/nelson-mandela.jpg" alt="Nelson Mandela" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/nelson-mandela">Nelson Mandela</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Nelson_Mandela_1994.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Kingkongphoto &amp; www.celebrity-photos.com from Laurel, CC BY-SA 2.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>In 1952 the ANC and the South African Indian Congress launched the Defiance Campaign against Unjust Laws: volunteers deliberately broke segregation laws, entering “whites only” facilities and defying curfews, and more than 8,000 were arrested. On 26 June 1955 the Congress of the People at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kliptown">Kliptown</a> adopted the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/freedom-charter">Freedom Charter</a>, and on 9 August 1956 about 20,000 women marched to the Union Buildings in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pretoria">Pretoria</a> against the extension of passes to women. In December 1956 the police arrested 156 leaders of the Congress Alliance for high treason; the Treason Trial dragged on until 1961, when the last of the accused were acquitted.</p><p>On 21 March 1960 the PAC called on Africans to leave their passes at home and offer themselves for arrest. At Sharpeville, near Vereeniging, police opened fire on the crowd outside the police station, killing 69 people, most of them shot in the back, and police also fired on protesters at Langa in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cape-town">Cape Town</a>. The government declared a state of emergency, detained thousands and banned the ANC and the PAC. In 1961 the Union of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-africa">South Africa</a> became the Republic of South <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a> and left the Commonwealth. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/umkhonto-we-sizwe">Umkhonto we Sizwe</a> began its sabotage campaign on 16 December 1961, and Poqo, linked to the PAC, killed white civilians and Africans it regarded as collaborators. After the police raided the movement’s hideout at Liliesleaf Farm in Rivonia in 1963, Mandela, already serving a prison sentence, told the court in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rivonia-trial">Rivonia Trial</a> that a free and democratic society was “an ideal for which I am prepared to die”; in June 1964 he and seven others were sentenced to life imprisonment.</p><p>Detention without trial, banning orders and torture broke the underground in the 1960s, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oliver-tambo">Oliver Tambo</a> led the ANC from exile. Resistance revived in the 1970s: Black Consciousness inspired a new generation, strikes in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/durban">Durban</a> in 1973 brought out tens of thousands of workers, and on 16 June 1976 police in Soweto fired on schoolchildren marching against the use of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afrikaans">Afrikaans</a> in their schools, killing Hector Pieterson among others. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soweto-uprising">Soweto uprising</a> spread across the country and left several hundred dead, and thousands of young people fled abroad, many to train as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guerrilla-warfare">guerrillas</a>. In September 1977 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/steve-biko">Steve Biko</a> died in police custody after severe beatings.</p><p>Abroad, a consumer boycott launched in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> in 1959 became the Anti-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/apartheid">Apartheid</a> Movement after Sharpeville. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-nations-general-assembly">United Nations General Assembly</a> called for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanctions">sanctions</a> in 1962, South Africa was barred from the Olympic Games from 1964 and expelled from the Olympic movement in 1970, protests disrupted its rugby and cricket tours, and after Biko’s death the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-nations-security-council">United Nations Security Council</a> imposed a mandatory arms embargo in 1977.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="people">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">People</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/desmond-tutu"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/desmond-tutu.jpg" alt="Desmond Tutu" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/desmond-tutu">Desmond Tutu</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Archbishop-Tutu-medium.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Benny Gool, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/albert-luthuli">Albert Luthuli</a> (c. 1898–1967), a Zulu chief deposed by the government for his politics, led the ANC from 1952 and received the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nobel-peace-prize">Nobel Peace Prize</a> for 1960. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nelson-mandela">Nelson Mandela</a> (1918–2013), a lawyer who opened a practice with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oliver-tambo">Oliver Tambo</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/johannesburg">Johannesburg</a> in 1952, was “volunteer-in-chief” of the Defiance Campaign and the first commander of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/umkhonto-we-sizwe">Umkhonto we Sizwe</a>, and in twenty-seven years in prison, eighteen of them on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/robben-island">Robben Island</a>, he became the world’s best-known political prisoner. Walter Sisulu, the ANC’s secretary-general and Mandela’s mentor, was imprisoned with him; Oliver Tambo (1917–1993) left the country in 1960 and led the ANC in exile for three decades. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/robert-sobukwe">Robert Sobukwe</a> (1924–1978), the PAC’s leader, was held on Robben Island for six years under a law passed to keep him there, then confined to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kimberley">Kimberley</a> until his death. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/steve-biko">Steve Biko</a> (1946–1977), a medical student, founded the South African Students’ Organisation in 1968 and gave Black Consciousness its voice.</p><p>Women were at the heart of the struggle. Lilian Ngoyi and Helen Joseph led the Women’s March of 1956, Albertina Sisulu became one of the presidents of the UDF, and Winnie Madikizela-Mandela, banished to a small town in the Free State from 1977 to 1985, kept her husband’s name before the world, though the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/truth-and-reconciliation-commission-south-africa">Truth and Reconciliation Commission</a> later held her politically and morally accountable for crimes committed by her bodyguards, the Mandela United Football Club. The churches gave the movement shelter and a voice: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/desmond-tutu">Desmond Tutu</a>, general secretary of the South African Council of Churches and from 1986 Archbishop of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cape-town">Cape Town</a>, received the Nobel Peace Prize in 1984, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afrikaners">Afrikaner</a> minister Beyers Naudé left the ministry of his church to found the Christian Institute in 1963. White opponents of apartheid included the communist lawyer Bram Fischer, who defended Mandela at Rivonia and died a prisoner, the writer Ruth First, killed by a parcel bomb sent by the security police to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maputo">Maputo</a> in 1982, and Helen Suzman, for thirteen years the only member of her party in parliament.</p><p>Abroad, the Anglican priest Trevor Huddleston, who had worked in Sophiatown until 1956, became president of the British Anti-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/apartheid">Apartheid</a> Movement, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/julius-nyerere">Julius Nyerere</a> of Tanzania and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kenneth-kaunda">Kenneth Kaunda</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zambia">Zambia</a> gave the exiled movements bases. Yet the movement’s strength lay with millions of ordinary people: the women who resisted the passes, the workers who built trade unions, the students of 1976, the township youth of the 1980s, and the prisoners, exiles and their families, who paid for resistance with their liberty and often their lives.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="outcomes">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Outcomes</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/f-w-de-klerk"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/f-w-de-klerk.jpg" alt="F. W. de Klerk" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/f-w-de-klerk">F. W. de Klerk</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:F.W._de_Klerk_wait_to_speak_in_Philadelphia,_Pennsylvania_LCCN2011634246_(cropped).tif" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Carol M. Highsmith, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>In the 1980s P. W. Botha’s government mixed reform with repression. Its constitution of 1983 gave coloureds and Indians separate chambers of parliament but excluded Africans, and its opponents formed the United Democratic Front (UDF), a coalition of hundreds of organisations, in August 1983; the Congress of South African Trade Unions followed in 1985. From September 1984 the townships rose in strikes and rent and school boycotts, and the ANC called for them to be made ungovernable. Under states of emergency in 1985 and 1986 troops occupied the townships, tens of thousands of people, many of them children, were detained, and security force death squads killed activists at home and abroad. MK’s bombs also killed civilians, as in 1983, when a car bomb outside air force headquarters in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pretoria">Pretoria</a> killed nineteen people, two of them the bombers; crowds killed hundreds of suspected collaborators, many by “necklacing” with burning tyres; and fighting between supporters of the UDF and of Inkatha killed thousands.</p><p>No side could win outright. In 1985 foreign banks refused to renew <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-africa">South Africa</a>’s loans, and in 1986 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states-congress">United States Congress</a> passed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/comprehensive-anti-apartheid-act">Comprehensive Anti-Apartheid Act</a> over the veto of President <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ronald-reagan">Ronald Reagan</a>, while European and Commonwealth countries also imposed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanctions">sanctions</a>. Ministers met Mandela in secret from 1985, and after the end of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cold-war">Cold War</a> had weakened the government’s claim to be a bulwark against communism, Botha’s successor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/f-w-de-klerk">F. W. de Klerk</a> unbanned the ANC, the PAC and the Communist Party on 2 February 1990; Mandela walked free on 11 February.</p><p>The years of negotiation were among the bloodiest of the conflict. Thousands died in fighting between ANC and Inkatha supporters, amid evidence of collusion by elements of the security forces, and in attacks by white extremists and by the PAC’s armed wing. The main apartheid laws were repealed in 1991, white voters backed negotiations in a referendum in March 1992, and when a white extremist murdered the ANC and Communist leader <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chris-hani">Chris Hani</a> in April 1993, Mandela’s appeal for calm helped to fix an election date. An interim constitution was agreed in November 1993, and on 27 April 1994 South Africans of all races voted together for the first time. The ANC won 62 per cent of the vote, and on 10 May Mandela was inaugurated as president of a Government of National Unity, with de Klerk and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thabo-mbeki">Thabo Mbeki</a> as his deputies.</p><p>The movement had won its central aim, the end of legal racial discrimination and votes for all, confirmed in the constitution of 1996, but not the redistribution of land and wealth promised in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/freedom-charter">Freedom Charter</a>, and the economic inequalities built under apartheid long outlasted it.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="legacy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Legacy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/robben-island"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/robben-island.jpg" alt="Robben Island" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/robben-island">Robben Island</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Robben_Island_-_Cape_Town,_South_Africa_(3883849594).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">South African Tourism from South Africa, CC BY 2.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The struggle against apartheid became one of the largest international solidarity movements of the twentieth century. Its consumer, sports and cultural boycotts, its campaigns for disinvestment by universities, churches and pension funds, and its work through trade unions and city councils became models for later campaigns, and the worldwide call to free Mandela made him a symbol of resistance to injustice. The British Anti-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/apartheid">Apartheid</a> Movement became Action for Southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a> in 1994.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-africa">South Africa</a>’s own reckoning took the form of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/truth-and-reconciliation-commission-south-africa">Truth and Reconciliation Commission</a>, chaired by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/desmond-tutu">Desmond Tutu</a>, which from 1996 heard thousands of victims and granted amnesty to perpetrators who made full disclosure of politically motivated crimes. Its report, presented in 1998, found the state responsible for most of the gross violations of human rights committed between 1960 and 1994, but also held the liberation movements, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/inkatha-freedom-party">Inkatha Freedom Party</a> and others accountable for killings and abuses, including torture and executions in ANC camps in exile. The commission became a model, and a subject of debate, for societies emerging from conflict elsewhere. South Africa marks the struggle in its public holidays, among them Human Rights Day on 21 March, the anniversary of Sharpeville, Youth Day on 16 June, National Women’s Day on 9 August and Freedom Day on 27 April, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/robben-island">Robben Island</a> has become a museum and a World Heritage Site.</p><p>Historians weigh the forces that ended apartheid differently. Some stress the mass resistance of the 1970s and 1980s, the trade unions, the UDF and the township uprisings; others the crisis of an economy short of skilled labour and foreign capital, the cost of war in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/angola">Angola</a>, the effect of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanctions">sanctions</a>, which remains debated, and the end of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cold-war">Cold War</a>. The armed struggle, militarily weak, is usually judged more important for its symbolic power. Studies of the exiled ANC, such as Stephen Ellis’s, have examined the influence of the Communist Party and the abuses in its camps, while critics have asked whether a settlement that protected property and the existing economy deserved the name of the “negotiated revolution” its admirers gave it.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>The anti-apartheid movement united Africans, Indians, coloureds and whites in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-africa">South Africa</a>, and supporters across the world, against the racial order imposed from 1948. Through defiance campaigns, strikes, uprisings, armed struggle and international boycotts and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanctions">sanctions</a>, at a heavy cost in lives on many sides, it brought the apartheid state to negotiate its own end, in the democratic election of 1994 and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nelson-mandela">Nelson Mandela</a>’s presidency.</em></p>

